      <h1 style="font-size: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; color: var(--text-head);">Best HRA Calculator India (2025): How to Maximize Your Tax Exemption</h1>

      <p style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        House Rent Allowance (HRA) is one of the most powerful tax saving options for salaried individuals in India under the old tax regime. But calculating the exact tax-exempt portion under Section 10(13A) can be confusing.
      </p>

      <p>In this guide, we review the rules for HRA calculation, the formula used by the <strong>best HRA calculators in India</strong>, and how the upcoming 8th Pay Commission changes HRA slab rates for government employees.</p>

      <div style="margin: 3rem 0; padding: 2rem; background: var(--bg-surface); border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.05);">
        <h2 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--text-head);">The HRA Exemption Formula (Section 10(13A))</h2>
        <p>Your HRA tax exemption is not the total amount you pay in rent. According to the Income Tax Act, the tax-free HRA is the <strong>minimum of these three values</strong>:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Actual HRA:</strong> The HRA allowance received from your employer.</li>
          <li><strong>50% or 40% of Basic Salary:</strong> 50% of (Basic Salary + Dearness Allowance) if you live in a metro city (Delhi, Mumbai, Kolkata, Chennai). 40% if you live in a non-metro city.</li>
          <li><strong>Excess Rent Paid:</strong> Actual rent paid minus 10% of your (Basic Salary + DA).</li>
        </ol>
        <p>The remaining amount of your HRA is added to your taxable income and taxed at your regular slab rates.</p>
      </div>

      <h2>Why Metro vs. Non-Metro Matters</h2>
      <p>Under the Income Tax rules, only four cities qualify for the 50% basic salary rate: <strong>Delhi, Mumbai, Kolkata, and Chennai</strong>. If you live in Bangalore, Hyderabad, Pune, Gurgaon, or Noida, you qualify only for the 40% rate, even though these are major metropolitan regions. This is a common point of confusion when using simple HRA calculators online.</p>

      <h2>8th Pay Commission HRA Rates</h2>
      <p>The 8th Pay Commission (proposed to take effect in January 2026) aims to revise the HRA slabs for government employees based on population census classes (X, Y, and Z cities):</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Class X Cities (Population > 50 Lakhs):</strong> HRA rate proposed to rise to <strong>30%</strong> of basic salary (up from 24%).</li>
        <li><strong>Class Y Cities (Population 5 - 50 Lakhs):</strong> HRA rate proposed to rise to <strong>20%</strong> of basic salary (up from 16%).</li>
        <li><strong>Class Z Cities (Population < 5 Lakhs):</strong> HRA rate proposed to rise to <strong>10%</strong> of basic salary (up from 8%).</li>
      </ul>

