      <h2>Tips to Maximize HRA Exemption</h2>
      <p>If your rent is less than 10% of your basic salary, your tax exemption on rent is zero. To make the most of it, consider these points:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Pay Rent to Parents:</strong> If you live with your parents, you can pay them rent, obtain rent receipts, and claim HRA. Your parents will declare the rent under "Income from House Property" and can claim a 30% standard deduction. Note: You cannot pay rent to your spouse and claim HRA.</li>
        <li><strong>PAN of Landlord:</strong> If your annual rent exceeds ₹1,00,000, you must submit the PAN card number of your landlord to your employer to prevent a tax deduction at source (TDS).</li>
        <li><strong>Shared Accommodation:</strong> If you share an apartment with roommates, split the rent. You can claim HRA relative to your share of the rent, provided the lease agreement lists you as a co-tenant.</li>
      </ul>

